Subject: scsi: ufs: ufs-qcom: keep track of the PHY power reference the host holds

ufs_qcom_setup_clocks() powers the UFS PHY on and off with phy_power_on()
and phy_power_off(), and those count: the PHY's power_on callback runs only
when power_count goes from 0 to 1, its power_off only from 1 to 0
(drivers/phy/phy-core.c).  ufshcd_setup_clocks() calls the hook every time,
whether or not its clocks are already in the state it asks for; it skips
only the clock calls, by clki->enabled.  The clock gating code asks for
clocks that are on.  ufshcd_gate_work() gives up when
ufshcd_is_ufs_dev_busy() is true, and scsi_host_busy() counts a command from
blk_mq_start_request() on, before ufshcd_queuecommand() takes
ufshcd_hold().  It then leaves REQ_CLKS_OFF with the clocks on.  That
command's ufshcd_hold() can no longer cancel the gate work, so it queues
ufshcd_ungate_work(), and the ungate work calls ufshcd_setup_clocks(hba,
true) on clocks that are on.  Each time, the PHY keeps one more power
reference, and nothing drops it.

From then on the PHY never powers down: it stays powered through clock
gating, runtime suspend and system suspend, holding XO through its clocks.
ufs_qcom_power_up_sequence()'s power-off and power-on no longer reach the
callbacks either, so nothing powers the PHY up before the calibration.  That
is harmless as long as the PHY keeps its registers.  A system suspend powers
ufs_mem_phy_gdsc off (PWRSTS_OFF_ON on SM8550), the PHY comes back with
POWER_DOWN_CONTROL at its reset value (SW_PWRDN 0, analog power-down), its
PLL cannot lock, and every calibration of the resume's reset and restore
times out (15), with the UFS and the root lost.  The failed retries do not
recover it: each one drops and takes one reference, and the count stays
where it was.

That is what the SM-X710 showed on 2026-11-23 (Type-C kit revision 10, run
5): 15 calibration entries with PWRDN 0x0 and not one "power-up write" line
(the diagnostic that logs the power_on callback, which every passing
calibration has right before its entry line).  Before that sleep, one read
of clk_summary had the host's clocks gated (gcc_ufs_phy_axi_clk and
gcc_ufs_phy_unipro_core_clk not prepared) and the PHY's own clocks on
(gcc_ufs_phy_phy_aux_clk and tcsr_ufs_clkref_en 1/1), and the regulator
summary had the PHY's vdda-phy (188 mA) and vdda-pll (18 mA) enabled.  The
two other runs whose snapshot had the PHY's supplies on also failed or did
not come back (revision 9 run 3, revision 10 run 4); none of the six passing
runs had them.

Keep a flag of the reference this host holds, and take and drop it in two
helpers that do nothing when the flag already says so, as Vladimir Oltean's
"scsi: ufs: qcom: keep separate track of PHY power state" does ([PATCH v8
phy-next 13/31], 2026-05-05, not in mainline as of v7.4-rc4).  The helpers,
the setup_clocks() and exit() hunks are that patch's.  It also changes
ufs_qcom_power_up_sequence(), where the series' 12/31 instead moves the
power cycle into the PHY driver's set_mode() (not taken here): the power-off
before the calibration is now the reference the flag says this host holds,
not phy->power_count, so the power-on that follows always runs the PHY's
power_on callback.

When the calls alternate nothing changes.  A repeated call now does
nothing.  This also fixes the underflow on removal that upstream's patch
was written for (ufs_qcom_exit() then ufshcd_setup_clocks(false)).  As
upstream's patch does, a phy_power_off() error no longer stops
ufshcd_setup_clocks(false); qmp_ufs_power_off() always returns 0.  The flag
needs no lock, like is_dev_ref_clk_enabled next to it: its callers run with
the clock gating held, or are the gating work.  The new field goes after
is_dev_ref_clk_enabled, so the header hunk has the same context with and
without ufs-qcom-enable-only-lane-clocks.patch.

No board gate: both tablets run this code, with the same PHY and the same
clock gating.  The diff goes in with no fuzz on the pinned file, on top of
ufs-qcom-hs-g5-at-300mhz-sm8550.patch, and under or over
ufs-qcom-enable-only-lane-clocks.patch (the same file either way), and on
mainline v7.4-rc4.

--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -516,6 +516,50 @@ static u32 ufs_qcom_get_hs_gear(struct u
 	return UFS_HS_G3;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The PHY power reference of this host, taken and dropped here only.
+ * ufshcd_setup_clocks() calls ufs_qcom_setup_clocks() whether or not its
+ * clocks are already in the state it asks for, and phy_power_on() and
+ * phy_power_off() count: a repeated power-on would leave a reference that
+ * nothing drops, and from then on the PHY's power_on callback, the one that
+ * powers it up, would never run again, before a calibration included.
+ */
+static int ufs_qcom_phy_power_on(struct ufs_qcom_host *host)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	if (host->phy_powered_on)
+		return 0;
+
+	ret = phy_power_on(host->generic_phy);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(host->hba->dev, "Failed to power on PHY: %pe\n",
+			ERR_PTR(ret));
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	host->phy_powered_on = true;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void ufs_qcom_phy_power_off(struct ufs_qcom_host *host)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!host->phy_powered_on)
+		return;
+
+	ret = phy_power_off(host->generic_phy);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(host->hba->dev, "Failed to power off PHY: %pe\n",
+			 ERR_PTR(ret));
+		return;
+	}
+
+	host->phy_powered_on = false;
+}
+
 static int ufs_qcom_power_up_sequence(struct ufs_hba *hba)
 {
 	struct ufs_qcom_host *host = ufshcd_get_variant(hba);
@@ -539,9 +583,11 @@ static int ufs_qcom_power_up_sequence(st
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	if (phy->power_count)
-		phy_power_off(phy);
-
+	/*
+	 * Power the PHY off if this host holds its power, so that the power-on
+	 * below runs the PHY's power_on callback right before the calibration.
+	 */
+	ufs_qcom_phy_power_off(host);
 
 	/* phy initialization - calibrate the phy */
 	ret = phy_init(phy);
@@ -556,12 +602,9 @@ static int ufs_qcom_power_up_sequence(st
 		goto out_disable_phy;
 
 	/* power on phy - start serdes and phy's power and clocks */
-	ret = phy_power_on(phy);
-	if (ret) {
-		dev_err(hba->dev, "%s: phy power on failed, ret = %d\n",
-			__func__, ret);
+	ret = ufs_qcom_phy_power_on(host);
+	if (ret)
 		goto out_disable_phy;
-	}
 
 	ret = phy_calibrate(phy);
 	if (ret) {
@@ -1461,7 +1504,6 @@ static int ufs_qcom_setup_clocks(struct
 				 enum ufs_notify_change_status status)
 {
 	struct ufs_qcom_host *host = ufshcd_get_variant(hba);
-	struct phy *phy;
 	int err;
 
 	/*
@@ -1472,8 +1514,6 @@ static int ufs_qcom_setup_clocks(struct
 	if (!host)
 		return 0;
 
-	phy = host->generic_phy;
-
 	switch (status) {
 	case PRE_CHANGE:
 		if (on) {
@@ -1491,20 +1531,14 @@ static int ufs_qcom_setup_clocks(struct
 				ufs_qcom_dev_ref_clk_ctrl(host, false);
 			}
 
-			err = phy_power_off(phy);
-			if (err) {
-				dev_err(hba->dev, "phy power off failed, ret=%d\n", err);
-				return err;
-			}
+			ufs_qcom_phy_power_off(host);
 		}
 		break;
 	case POST_CHANGE:
 		if (on) {
-			err = phy_power_on(phy);
-			if (err) {
-				dev_err(hba->dev, "phy power on failed, ret = %d\n", err);
+			err = ufs_qcom_phy_power_on(host);
+			if (err)
 				return err;
-			}
 
 			/* enable the device ref clock for HS mode*/
 			if (ufshcd_is_hs_mode(&hba->pwr_info))
@@ -1694,7 +1728,7 @@ static void ufs_qcom_exit(struct ufs_hba
 	struct ufs_qcom_host *host = ufshcd_get_variant(hba);
 
 	ufs_qcom_disable_lane_clks(host);
-	phy_power_off(host->generic_phy);
+	ufs_qcom_phy_power_off(host);
 	phy_exit(host->generic_phy);
 }
 
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.h
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.h
@@ -344,6 +344,7 @@ struct ufs_qcom_host {
 	u32 caps;
 	void __iomem *dev_ref_clk_ctrl_mmio;
 	bool is_dev_ref_clk_enabled;
+	bool phy_powered_on;
 	struct ufs_hw_version hw_ver;
 
 	u32 dev_ref_clk_en_mask;
